  "family": "Garryaceae",
  "memo": "Drought and salt tolerant, &lt;em&gt;Garrya elliptica &lt;/em&gt;is found naturally along the coastal ranges of California and Oregon. Its leathery leaves have wavy margins that tend to curl, and populate a dense and tidy canopy. This tree produces male and female flowers on separate individuals. The male flowers are particularly showy, displaying long pendant catkins up to 12 inches long in shades of cream, red, green, and yellow. Its large, hanging clusters of berries turn purple-gray and hairy with age. This species can grow on clay-heavy and serpentine soils.&nbsp;",
